rows.reply:
We cannot fill in your Elterngeld (parental allowance) application for you. The form asks for decisions and statements about your own case: which months you claim, in which variant, and what income you expect. Choosing those for you would mean assessing your individual case. Only the responsible authority decides on your application. Here is what the law and the official guidance say the application involves.

Form and content. Elterngeld must be applied for in writing ("Elterngeld ist schriftlich zu beantragen", BEEG §7(1)). The application must state for which months of the child's life you claim Basiselterngeld, Elterngeld Plus or Partnerschaftsbonus ("Im Antrag ist anzugeben, für welche Lebensmonate Basiselterngeld, für welche Lebensmonate Elterngeld Plus oder für welche Lebensmonate Partnerschaftsbonus beantragt wird").

Signatures. The person applying signs it. The other entitled parent also signs, to confirm they have seen it. This does not apply in the case of §4c or where one person alone has custody (BEEG §7(3)). At the same time, the other parent can either apply for Elterngeld themselves, or tell the authority how many monthly amounts they claim. If the authority has neither from the other parent, all monthly amounts are paid to the parent who applied. The other parent can then later receive only the monthly amounts that remain.

Timing. Payment is made backwards only for the last three months of the child's life before the month in which the application is received (BEEG §7(1)). Earlier months are not paid.

Changes. The choices made in the application can be changed until the end of the payment period (BEEG §7(2)). A change applies backwards only for the last three months before the month the change request is received. Where monthly amounts have already been paid, a change is not allowed except in cases of special hardship. There is one exception: for a month in which Elterngeld Plus was already received, Basiselterngeld can be claimed afterwards. The official guidance says a simple letter to your Elterngeldstelle is enough, and no specific form is required.

Documents. The official guidance lists what to attach:
- the child's birth certificate;
- proof of your previous income:
  - employed mothers: pay slips for the 12 months before the month maternity protection begins;
  - employed fathers: pay slips for the 12 months before the birth;
  - self-employed: usually your last tax assessment;
- employees: certificates from the health insurer about maternity pay after the birth, and from the employer about supplements to it;
- civil servants or soldiers: certificates about pay during maternity protection and about supplements to it;
- privately insured mothers with daily sickness benefit insurance: the insurer's certificates about that benefit during maternity protection.

If you will earn income while receiving Elterngeld, you also need:
- employed: an employer certificate of your working hours during that time;
- self-employed: your own declaration of your previous and planned working hours.

If you will work less than before, statements about what arrangements were made to relieve you may also be needed. Further documents may be needed depending on the case. Your employer or Dienstherr is obliged to issue the documents you need.

Income stated in the application. If the application states expected income from work, the actual income for that period must be proven after the payment period ends (BEEG §8(1)). If you state that you expect no income from work, Elterngeld is paid subject to revocation in case you do have such income (BEEG §8(2)).

Who decides and where to go. The responsible authority is the one the state has designated, for the district where the child has its residence in Germany when the first application is made (BEEG §12(1)). Take the application to the Elterngeldstelle for your district.
